.\"	@(#)gets.3s	6.1 (Berkeley) 5/15/85
.\"
.TH GETS 3S  "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
gets, fgets \- get a string from a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
#include <stdio.h>
.PP
.ft B
char *gets(char *\fIs\fP);
.PP
.ft B
char *fgets(char *\fIs\fP, int \fIn\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gets
reads a string into
.I s
from the standard input stream
.IR stdin , 
until end-of-file is encountered or a new-line 
character is read.
The string is terminated by a newline
character, which is replaced in
.I s
by a null character.
.PP
.I Fgets
reads 
.IR n \-1
characters, or up through a newline
character, whichever comes first,
from the
.I stream
into the string
.IR s .
No additional characters are read after a new-line character
(which is retained) or after end-of-file.
The last character read into
.I s
is followed by a null character.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
.I Fgets
and
.I gets
returns \fIs\fR if successful.  If unsuccessful, a null pointer
is returned and an error code is stored in \fIerrno\fR.
.SH "ERRORS"
The underlying function of
.I fgets
and
.I gets
is \fIread\fR(2).
The error conditions specified for
.IR read (2)
apply to 
.IR fgets
and
.I gets.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
read(2),
puts(3S),
getc(3S),
scanf(3S),
fread(3S),
ferror(3S)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Gets
and
.I fgets
return the constant pointer 
.SM
.B NULL
upon end of file or error.
.SH BUGS
.I Gets
deletes a newline,
.I fgets
keeps it,
all in the name of backward compatibility.
